page cmd_record {
  title = "vmlab record"
  chapter_header "vmlab record" {
    kicker       = "Commands · reference"
    reading_time = "5 min read"
    updated      = "2026-10-21"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab record` records a VM's console to an MP4 or WebM file. The lab
    daemon owns the recording: it carries on after this command returns,
    survives guest reboots, `vm restart` and snapshot restores, and is
    finalised to a playable file when you stop it or when the machine goes
    away. See [Console recording](guide_recording) for what the file holds,
    the pointer rule and idle compression.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab record <COMMAND>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Subcommand" | "Meaning" |
      | "`start`" | "Start recording a VM's console to an MP4 or WebM file." |
      | "`stop`" | "Stop a VM's recording and finalise the file." |
      | "`status`" | "List live recordings, for one VM or the whole lab." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    Every subcommand takes a VM reference of the form `[lab/]vm`. A bare name
    is resolved against the lab in the current directory, and the lab daemon
    is started if none is running. The qualified form addresses a lab that is
    already running from any directory. Recording needs `ffmpeg` on the host;
    see "What it needs" below.
  DOC

  h2 "vmlab record start"

  code sh { source = "vmlab record start [OPTIONS] <VM>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`<VM>`" | "The VM, as `[lab/]vm`." |
      | "`-o`, `--output <PATH>`" | "Output file; the extension picks the format, `.mp4` (H.264) or `.webm` (VP9). A relative path resolves against the shell's working directory. Default: `.vmlab/recordings/<vm>-<UTC timestamp>.mp4` in the lab." |
      | "`--fps <N>`" | "Frames per second, 1 to 120. Default 30." |
      | "`--size <WxH>`" | "Output frame size, e.g. `1280x720`. Every frame is scaled with its aspect kept and letterboxed into it. Both dimensions even, between 16 and 7680x4320. Default `1920x1080`." |
      | "`--compress-idle[=SECS]`" | "Cut idle stretches longer than `SECS` to half a second. With no figure, 2. The `=` is required when a figure is given." |
      | "`--timeline`" | "Write `<output>.events.json` beside the video, listing every input vmlab sent in video time." |
      | "`--max-duration <DURATION>`" | "Finalise the recording once the video is this long: seconds (`90`), or a figure with `s`, `m`, `h` units, chained (`90s`, `5m`, `1h30m`)." |
      | "`--json`" | "Print the daemon's reply as JSON." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    The CLI refuses a bad extension, including `.gif`, and a malformed
    `--max-duration` before it contacts a daemon. The daemon then checks, in
    this order: that the machine has a display (a container has none), that
    it is not already recording, that the size and rate are acceptable, and
    that the host has an `ffmpeg` with the format's encoder. Only then does
    it connect to the VM's VNC socket and start ffmpeg. On success it prints
    one line with the options in force, defaults filled in, and emits
    `record.started`:
  DOC

  code text { source = "recording demo/win01 to /home/me/demo/.vmlab/recordings/win01-20261021T101500.000.mp4 (30 fps, 1920x1080)" }

  p <<DOC
    An idle stretch is a run of frame intervals in which the framebuffer
    changed by at most 64x64 pixels and vmlab sent no input; a blinking caret
    or a clock does not break it, and a click on a static screen is never
    cut. The recording also ends on its own when `--max-duration` of video
    has been written, and when the `host.disk_low` watchdog fires. Either
    way the file is finalised and `record.stopped` names the reason.
  DOC

  h2 "vmlab record stop"

  code sh { source = "vmlab record stop [OPTIONS] <VM>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`<VM>`" | "The VM, as `[lab/]vm`." |
      | "`--json`" | "Print the daemon's reply as JSON." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    Closes ffmpeg's input, lets it write the trailer and waits for it, then
    prints the path and the length of the video. With `--timeline` the
    sidecar's path follows on a second line. A recording that had already
    ended on its own adds a line saying why. A machine that is not recording
    is `not_found`: `machine X is not recording`.
  DOC

  code text { source = <<TXT
stopped recording demo/win01: /home/me/demo/.vmlab/recordings/win01-20261021T101500.000.mp4 (1m35s of video)
timeline: /home/me/demo/.vmlab/recordings/win01-20261021T101500.000.mp4.events.json
TXT
  }

  h2 "vmlab record status"

  code sh { source = "vmlab record status [OPTIONS] [VM]" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`[VM]`" | "The VM, as `[lab/]vm`. Omit it for every machine in the lab." |
      | "`--json`" | "Print the daemon's reply as a JSON array." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    One row per live recording: `MACHINE`, `ELAPSED` on the wall clock,
    `STARTED` in UTC, `PATH` and the options in force. With nothing live it
    prints `no recordings`. A recording that ended on its own is finalised
    here and no longer listed. [vmlab status](cmd_status) shows the same
    entries in its `RECORDING` section, and `vmlab status --json` carries
    each as a `recording` object on its machine: `machine`, `path`,
    `started_at`, `elapsed_secs`, `options`.
  DOC

  code text { source = <<TXT
MACHINE  ELAPSED  STARTED              PATH  (OPTIONS)
win01      1m35s  2026-10-21 10:15:00  /home/me/demo/.vmlab/recordings/win01-20261021T101500.000.mp4  (30 fps, 1920x1080, compress-idle 2s)
TXT
  }

  h2 "--record on script, up, vm start and vm restart"

  code sh { source = <<SH
vmlab script   [--record <VM=PATH>]... [RECORD OPTIONS] <SCRIPT>
vmlab up       [--record <VM=PATH>]... [RECORD OPTIONS] [VMS]...
vmlab vm start [--record <VM=PATH>]... [RECORD OPTIONS] <VM>
vmlab vm restart [--record <VM=PATH>]... [RECORD OPTIONS] <VM>
SH
  }

  p <<DOC
    `--record <vm>=<path>` asks the verb to record a VM alongside its own
    work. It repeats, once per VM, and a VM named twice is refused. The
    path is required and its extension picks the format as for `-o`; a
    relative path resolves against the shell's working directory. `--fps`,
    `--size`, `--compress-idle`, `--timeline` and `--max-duration` are
    accepted as above and apply to every recording the command line names.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    On `up`, `vm start` and `vm restart` the recording starts with the boot:
    QEMU starts paused, the recorder attaches, then the VM continues, so the
    video opens on the firmware's first frame. Every `--record` is checked
    before anything boots and one refusal arms none; the recordings then
    outlive the verb, and it prints `recording <vm> to <path>` once it is
    done. A `--record` for a VM that was already running, so that nothing
    booted, records it from now. One whose VM failed to start fails the verb
    with `machine X did not start, so its recording was not started`. A
    `vm restart` of a VM that is already recording is a `conflict`, because
    the existing recording follows the restart on its own.
  DOC

  p <<DOC
    On `script` the recordings last exactly the run: they start before the
    script, are finalised when it returns, whether it succeeded or failed,
    and the verb prints `recorded <vm> to <path>` at the end.
  DOC

  h2 "What it needs"

  p <<DOC
    The host needs `ffmpeg` built with `libx264` for `.mp4` or `libvpx-vp9`
    for `.webm`. vmlab runs the `ffmpeg` on `PATH`, or the one the `ffmpeg`
    key in the [host config](ref_host) names, and reads its `-encoders`
    list. The check runs when a recording starts and at no other time, so
    `vmlab up` without `--record` never looks for ffmpeg. The refusals:
  DOC

  code text { source = <<TXT
recording needs ffmpeg: none found on PATH, and the host config (config.wcl) names none under `ffmpeg`
recording a .mp4 needs the `libx264` encoder, which /usr/bin/ffmpeg -encoders does not list; install an ffmpeg built with it, or point the host config's `ffmpeg` key at one
TXT
  }

  h2 "Examples"

  p "Record a VM until you stop it, with the default name under `.vmlab/recordings/`:"
  code sh { source = <<SH
vmlab record start win01
vmlab record status
vmlab record stop win01
SH
  }

  p "A short, idle-compressed clip with a timeline, capped at five minutes of video:"
  code sh { source = "vmlab record start win01 -o demo.webm --size 1280x720 --compress-idle --timeline --max-duration 5m" }

  p "Record a boot from the firmware's first frame:"
  code sh { source = "vmlab vm restart win01 --record win01=boot.mp4 --compress-idle=5" }

  p "Record two machines for exactly as long as a script runs:"
  code sh { source = "vmlab script scripts/install.ws --record dc01=dc01.mp4 --record client01=client01.mp4 --timeline" }

  h2 "Exit status"

  p <<DOC
    Exit status is 0 on success. Exit 4 (`not_found`) means the lab declares
    no machine by that name, or, for `stop`, that the machine is not
    recording. Exit 5 (`conflict`) means the machine is already recording;
    the message names the live file and `vmlab record stop <vm>`. Exit 6
    (`unsupported`) means the machine has no display, which is every
    container. Exit 2 (`invalid_argument`) means the daemon refused the
    path, size or rate: a size with an odd dimension or outside 16 to
    7680x4320, a rate outside 1 to 120, or a relative path that reached it.
    Exit 1 (`failed`) means no `ffmpeg` or no encoder, a VM named by
    `--record` that did not start, or ffmpeg failing while the file was
    finalised. A bad extension or a malformed `--max-duration` is refused
    by the CLI before any request is sent, with a message and no code, and
    a usage error the argument parser rejects exits 2.
  DOC
}
